.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
.Dd October 29, 2026
.Dt NOQTIS-SEAL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm noqtis-seal
.Nd seal a folder of files into a signed capsule
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm noqtis seal
.Fl k Ar key
.Op Fl p Ar key.pub
.Op Fl m Ar json-file
.Op Fl -tsa Ar url ...
.Op Fl -anchor Ar der-file ...
.Ar dir
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm noqtis seal
turns the folder
.Ar dir
into a capsule
.Pq Xr noqtis-capsule 5 .
It lists every regular file under
.Ar dir ,
computes each file's size, SHA-256 and SHA3-256, writes the manifest
.Pa noqtis.json
(canonical JSON, RFC 8785), signs its exact bytes with the secret key in
.Ar key
under the context string
.Ql noqtis-capsule-1 ,
and writes the seal
.Pa noqtis.sig .
An existing
.Pa noqtis.json
and
.Pa noqtis.sig
at the top of
.Ar dir
are left out of the listing and replaced.
On success it prints
.Dl sealed Ar dir No with Ar alg
.Pp
With
.Fl -tsa ,
it then asks a time-stamping authority (TSA) for an RFC 3161 timestamp
over the seal's signature and stores the token in
.Pa noqtis.sig
.Pq Xr nqtsput 3 .
The request
.Pq Xr nqtsreq 3
holds only the SHA-256 of the signature, a random nonce and a request
for the TSA's certificate; it is sent by an HTTP/1.1 POST
.Pq Ql application/timestamp-query ,
and the reply must be a granted token over that signature with that
nonce, signed by a TSA certificate valid at its genTime.
It prints
.Dl timestamp from Ar url : No genTime Ar time
and the summary line ends in
.Ql and a timestamp .
A seal holds one timestamp: give
.Fl -tsa
again for fallbacks, tried in order until one gives a token that checks.
When none does, the new
.Pa noqtis.json
and
.Pa noqtis.sig
are removed, so the folder is never left sealed without the timestamp
that was asked for, and the exit status is 2.
An interrupt while it waits (Ctrl-C, SIGTERM, SIGHUP) removes them too;
only SIGKILL or a power cut can leave the folder sealed without one.
A TSA has 120 seconds in all for one request, the name lookup included
(30 seconds at most for any one step).
.Pp
The folder must not hold a noqtis secret key (a file that starts with
.Ql noqtis-key-1 <alg> sec ) ,
which would ship with the capsule: the seal is refused, with exit status 2.
.Pp
The folder must hold only regular files and folders with portable names.
Nothing is written, and the exit status is 2, when it holds a symbolic
link, a Windows reparse point, a device, pipe or socket, a name that is
empty, holds a control character,
.Ql / ,
.Ql \e
or
.Ql \&: ,
is not valid UTF-8, or two paths that differ only in ASCII case; or when it
exceeds a limit: more than 2,000,000 files, a path over 4096 bytes, folders
more than 64 levels deep, a manifest over 512 MiB, or a file over
2^53\-1 bytes.
.Pp
The creation time in the manifest is
.Ev SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
when it is set (a decimal number of seconds), otherwise the clock.
.Pp
The secret key and the public key must be one pair: the new seal is
verified before it is written, and a key pair that does not match is
refused.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl k Ar key , Fl -key Ns = Ns Ar key
A secret signature key file (ML-DSA or SLH-DSA).
.It Fl p Ar key.pub , Fl -pub Ns = Ns Ar key.pub
The matching public key, stored in the seal.
Default:
.Ar key Ns Pa .pub .
.It Fl m Ar json-file , Fl -meta Ns = Ns Ar json-file
A JSON object (at most 1 MiB) stored, in canonical form, as the
manifest's
.Ql meta
field and covered by the signature.
noqtis does not interpret it.
.It Fl -tsa Ar url
An RFC 3161 time-stamping authority, as
.Ql http:// Ns Ar host Ns Oo : Ns Ar port Oc Ns Op / Ns Ar path ,
up to 32 times.
Only plain HTTP: the token is signed by the TSA, so TLS would add no
integrity, and noqtis links no TLS library;
.Ql https://
is refused with that said.
Most public TSAs answer on
.Ql http:// .
There is no proxy support.
Each connection, read and write waits at most 30 seconds.
.It Fl -anchor Ar der-file
The TSA's root or issuing certificate (DER), up to 32: the token must
then also have a certificate path to one of them, checked as
.Xr noqtis-check 1
.Fl -anchor
checks it.
Without it the reply is checked, but not whether the TSA is one to
trust, and a note on standard error says so.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
Seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z to record as the creation time,
for reproducible capsules.
A value that is not a plain decimal number is refused.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Ar dir Ns Pa /noqtis.json
the manifest
.It Ar dir Ns Pa /noqtis.sig
the seal
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 when the capsule was written (with its timestamp, when
.Fl -tsa
was given); 2 on a usage error, keys that are not a signature key pair,
an unsealable folder, a limit, a file that cannot be read or written, or
no TSA that gave a timestamp.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ noqtis keygen -a ML-DSA-65 -o me
ML-DSA-65 key pair: me (secret), me.pub (public)
$ echo '{"project":"demo"}' > meta.json
$ noqtis seal -k me -m meta.json results
sealed results with ML-DSA-65
$ ls results
data  noqtis.json  noqtis.sig  report.txt
.Ed
.Pp
A reproducible capsule:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=1767225600 noqtis seal -k me results
sealed results with ML-DSA-65
.Ed
.Pp
With a timestamp from one of two authorities, checked at seal time:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ noqtis seal -k me --tsa http://timestamp.digicert.com \e
    --tsa http://timestamp.sectigo.com \e
    --anchor digicert.der --anchor sectigo.der results
timestamp from http://timestamp.digicert.com: genTime 2026-10-29T09:34:03Z, verified under a TSA anchor
sealed results with ML-DSA-65 and a timestamp
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis 1 ,
.Xr noqtis-check 1 ,
.Xr noqtis-keygen 1 ,
.Xr nqseal 3 ,
.Xr nqtsput 3 ,
.Xr noqtis-capsule 5
.Sh STANDARDS
.Rs
.%R RFC 3161
.%T Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Time-Stamp Protocol (TSP)
.%D August 2001
.Re
